                    <blockquote> It’s okay to take a closer look or even take some photos together. Just make sure that we don’t end up harassing them.</blockquote>
                    <p><span class="firstcharacter">A</span>n encounter with sea turtles will always be a special moment for every divers. They are likely to show up at various diving destinations in the tropics, and are usually tolerant of human accompaniance. The three mostly seen sea turtles are hawksbills, loggerheads and greens. They could be found in tropical waters worldwide, and often swim around the shore.</p>
                    <p>What should we do when we encounter these sea gliders? It’s okay to take a closer look or even take some photos together. Just make sure that we don’t end up harassing them. When they feel threatened, a green turtle could sprint up to 30 kilometres an hour. In this way, it is a little bit tricky to swim around the turtles. </p>
                    <p>Veteran divers will know that swimming alongside, in parallel, will help us to ensure that we are not in pursuit of them. It shows friendly gestures. After a few moments, we can begin to ease in closer while maintaining the same side-by-side position. Sometimes we’ll be able to get closer, yet, if the turtle speeds up, let it go and don’t chase.</p>
                    <p>When a turtle is resting, it can rest up to 6 hours underwater and it is best not to disturb them. It is amazing when we talk about their lung capability. Turtles have large lungs, which not only provides an ample reserve of oxygen, it also allows them to dive to greater depths without fear running out of oxygen. This enables them to swim underwater for up to 10 minutes. A great deal knowing that they are not amphibians, but reptiles.</p>
                    <p>Do you know that some sea turtles are vegetarian? Green turtles for example stop nibbling on crabs, worms and sponges and eat algae and seagrass instead. Hence, when you are around this plantation, chances of seeing them are higher. Wouldn’t it be fun to experience this underwater creature grazing through their food?</p>
                    <p>Ready to pack your bag and dive with these cute sea creatures? Turtles could be found all around the year in Bali. If you time it right, you can even enjoy the phenomenon of releasing the turtle at Kuta Beach. Keep in mind that we should not touch the turtles while swimming as we might transfer bacteria and as a guest, we should respect their home, which is the sea we are diving in. What are you waiting for? Book your trip now!</p>
